<p>
    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Landstill grinds out slow, incremental
    advantage and usually doesn't have many blue sources untapped at once.
    Your plan is to sit back, build up resources, and then overload their
    blue mana in a single turn. They can counter a test spell or two, but if
    you go off when they only have 3 or 4 open blue sources, your lethal <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
    > resolves as the last spell on the stack.
</p>
<p>
    <strong
        >Tactical Tools (Chant & Abeyance vs. Mana Short):</strong
    > After board, lean on your cheap interaction
    like <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Orim's Chant">Orim's Chant</a
    > and <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
    > over pricier options like <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Mana Short">Mana Short</a
    >. At one and two mana, Chant and Abeyance are cheap "must-counter"
    threats that don't eat into your own mana. Best of all, resolving one
    shuts off free interaction like <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Foil">Foil</a
    > or <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Thwart"
        >Thwart</a
    >—which Mana Short can't stop—and Abeyance even draws you a card and
    locks out <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Mishra's Factory">Mishra's Factory</a
    > and cycling activations.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Deploying Meddling Mage:</strong> When deploying an early
    <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
    > on turn 2, think about your turn 3 and 4 plays before you cast it.
    If you're setting up a combo turn and just want to strip a counter or
    tie up their mana, name
    <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
        >Counterspell</a
    >. If you'd rather keep the body around and start attacking, name <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
        >Swords to Plowshares</a
    > or their main card draw engine.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Sideboard Execution:</strong> Get your permanent answers down
    early—bring in your 4th <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
    > and copies of <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
    >. A Seal on the battlefield stops them from resolving a <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Standstill">Standstill</a
    >, and Tsabo's Web hits both their mana and their Mishra's
    Factory clock. <em
        >Note:</em
    > Don't bring in <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Deep Analysis">Deep Analysis</a
    > against Landstill or UW Dreadnought lists running Fling—paying
    3 life on your own is way too risky when they can just burn you
    out with it.
</p>
